APS-017 — Policy Federation

Document ID: APS-017
Title: Policy Federation
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Policy Federation Model (CPFM) for the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Canonical Policy Federation Model establishes the principles, architecture, governance, trust relationships, and interoperability mechanisms that enable multiple independent Atlas governance authorities to cooperate while preserving autonomy, accountability, semantic consistency, and engineering integrity.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Federation architecture
Federation authorities
Trust relationships
Cross-domain governance
Policy exchange
Delegated authority
Federation governance
Federation lifecycle

This specification applies to every federated Atlas governance environment.

3. Canonical Definition

Policy Federation is the governed cooperation of two or more autonomous Atlas governance authorities through defined trust relationships and federation agreements while preserving the independent governance authority of each participant.

Federation coordinates governance.

It SHALL NOT eliminate organizational sovereignty.

4. Objectives

The Policy Federation Model SHALL:

Enable cross-organization governance.
Preserve governance autonomy.
Enable trusted policy exchange.
Preserve semantic consistency.
Support regulatory cooperation.
Enable scalable governance.
Support distributed decision-making.
Maintain traceability.
5. Federation Principles

Every federation SHALL satisfy the following principles.

Sovereignty

Each federation participant SHALL retain governance authority over its own policies.

Explicit Trust

Trust relationships SHALL be explicitly established.

Implicit trust SHALL NOT exist.

Semantic Consistency

Federated policies SHALL preserve canonical Atlas semantics.

Mutual Accountability

Federation participants SHALL remain accountable for their published policies.

Traceability

Cross-domain policy interactions SHALL remain permanently traceable.

Governed Participation

Participation SHALL require an approved federation agreement.

6. Federation Participants

Atlas defines the following canonical participant types.

Participant	Description
Global Atlas Authority	Maintains constitutional governance
National Authority	Country-level governance
Organization Authority	Enterprise governance
Domain Authority	Discipline-specific governance
Project Authority	Project governance
Regulatory Authority	External regulatory organization

Additional participant types MAY be introduced through future profiles.

7. Federation Agreements

Every federation SHALL define:

Property	Description
Federation ID	Unique identifier
Participating Authorities	Federation members
Trust Model	Trust relationships
Governance Scope	Federated responsibilities
Policy Exchange Rules	Sharing constraints
Delegation Rules	Authority delegation
Review Schedule	Periodic review

Federation agreements SHALL be governed Canonical Atlas Objects.

8. Trust Relationships

Atlas defines the following canonical trust relationships.

Relationship	Description
Full Trust	Policies accepted without additional review
Conditional Trust	Subject to validation
Limited Trust	Restricted policy exchange
Observation	Visibility without authority
No Trust	Exchange prohibited

Trust relationships SHALL be directional unless explicitly declared reciprocal.

9. Policy Exchange

Federated participants MAY exchange:

Policy Definitions
Policy Packages
Policy Releases
Policy Baselines
Compliance Reports
Governance Decisions
Exception Records
Federation Agreements

Exchange SHALL preserve policy identity and governance metadata.

10. Delegated Authority

Federation MAY delegate authority for:

Policy review
Policy approval
Compliance assessment
Audit activities
Distribution
Emergency governance

Delegation SHALL define:

Delegating authority
Receiving authority
Delegated scope
Effective period
Revocation conditions

Delegation SHALL NOT transfer constitutional authority.

11. Cross-Domain Governance

Federation SHALL support governance across:

Organizations
Projects
Engineering disciplines
Regulatory jurisdictions
Geographic regions
Infrastructure ecosystems

Cross-domain governance SHALL preserve local governance where applicable.

12. Federation Lifecycle

Every federation SHALL support:

